In class activity - Think Like an AI Designer

1. For the problem identified in previous assignment , Please complete the following
What are the features?
   ANSWER: 
   Cancer type
   Stage at diagnosis
   Age at onset
   Gender
   Number of chemo sessions
   Number of radiation sessions
   Whether the operation was donated or charged

2. What is the label?
   ANSWER: What outcome should the model learn to predict?
   Treatment outcome — Success or Failure

3. What would training data contain?
   ANSWER: The only way to have cases with all the features mentioned above is to use past patient records
   together with the actual outcome already being known, just as in real cases where it is already known
   whether or not the treatment worked.

4. Why should testing data be kept separate?
   ANSWER: Because if you try the model on the same data that it was trained on, it will simply be able to
   'memorize' the answers and appear a lot better than it really is. The only way to actually find out whether
   the model can deal with patients it has never seen before is to keep the test data separate.

5. What could be a false positive?
   ANSWER: The model concludes that a patient is high-risk (it predicts failure) when in fact the patient turns out all right.
   This means that unnecessary extra monitoring or sessions is carried out that was not really needed and may unreasonably scare
   the patient and their family.

6. What could be a false negative?
   ANSWER: When the model predicts that a patient will make a full recovery an outcome it labels as a success the treatment
   still ends in failure. This is more problematic since the patient is not identified in time for early intervention,
   which is, in fact, the entire purpose of developing the model.

7. Would precision or recall be more important?
   ANSWER: Memories. It is far worse to fail to identify a high-risk patient than to identify a patient who is okay.
   A false positive is just a waste of some resources, but a false negative is someone who needed help not being
   flagged in time. So I'd rather over-flag a little with the model, than miss people, that's what recall is for.

Explain your reasoning.

8. What types of poor-quality data could affect this model?
   ANSWER: Patients referred late from tribal districts or former FATA areas have missing/incomplete staging information. 
   Registry data and operations data do not match/are formatted differently. Not enough cases of rarer cancer types.
   Results only tracked in aggregate, not by patient.
   Data is skewed toward patients who come in earlier and have cleaner records

9. What could cause this model to overfit?
   ANSWER: Too few cases for rare cancer types versus the number of features I am using. The model is too complex
   for the amount of data I have. The model is not using a proper test set or cross validation, it is overfitting
   to random patterns specific to this hospital/time period that would not generalize to new patients.


10. What would make you trust—or not trust—the model's predictions?
   ANSWER: I'd have more confidence in it if it were consistent across different patient groups, not just those with cleaner
   data, and if it had been tested on data it hadn't seen before. I’d also like the predicted probabilities to actually map
   to real outcomes, and a doctor to be able to confirm that the risk factors flagged make medical sense. I would be less
   trusting if it did significantly worse for underserved/late-referred patients (since that’s the group with the messiest data),
   if I couldn’t understand why it flagged someone, or if it was never tested outside the data it was trained on.




